target/arm: tomacco-bti=off, a CPU without FEAT_BTI.

-cpu max implements FEAT_BTI, which the iOS guest never uses (Apple's
arm64e code relies on pointer authentication; no page is ever guarded),
but which TCG pays for on every branch: BR and BLR store BTYPE, a BR
through a register other than x16/x17 calls helper_guarded_page_br, and
BTYPE is part of every A64 TB's flags, so one address entered by BLR
(BTYPE 2), BR x16 (1) and fall-through or RET (0) is up to three separate
translations, jump-cache entries and lookups.

The CPU property tomacco-bti (default on: unchanged) clears
ID_AA64PFR1_EL1.BT at realize when off, so the guest sees, and TCG
implements, no BTI. Guest-visible: the ID register changes; only use it
where the guest is known not to enable BTI (run_ios_paravirt.py --bti off).

tests/engine/branch-bench.S with qemu-tcg-jump-cache and
qemu-arm-jump-probe (ns per iteration, best of 7): msgsend 8.8 -> 5.4,
blr 8.1 -> 4.5, wide 56.6 -> 43.2, flush 67.5 -> 57.9, smc 87.7 -> 80.3;
same checksum.

diff --git a/target/arm/cpu.c b/target/arm/cpu.c
--- a/target/arm/cpu.c
+++ b/target/arm/cpu.c
@@ -2293,6 +2293,17 @@
 #endif
     }
 
+    /*
+     * tomacco-bti=off removes FEAT_BTI (Apple's cores have none; the guest
+     * never marks a page guarded). With it, every BR/BLR stores BTYPE, a
+     * BR through other than x16/x17 calls a guarded-page helper, and BTYPE
+     * is part of every TB's flags, so one address entered by BLR (2), BR
+     * x16 (1) and fallthrough (0) is three translations.
+     */
+    if (!cpu->tomacco_bti) {
+        FIELD_DP64_IDREG(isar, ID_AA64PFR1, BT, 0);
+    }
+
 #ifndef CONFIG_USER_ONLY
